  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nAlthough a few deeper showers attempted to develop within the \ndeeply mixed boundary layer in ern OH, where dewpoints are in \nthe mid 60s, east of the WV border is likely to remain dry \nthrough at least early Sat as a result of lower dewpoints and \nhigh pressure moving across the region. A digging shortwave \ntrough in the central CONUS is expected to advance ewd tonight \ninto Sat, spreading mid- and upper-level clouds into the upper\nOH Valley thru Sat.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nIncreasing moisture and ascent associated with a lead shortwave\ntrough should return shower and thunderstorm chances Sat \nafternoon across Ohio, overspreading the remainder of the area \nSat night.\n\nPeriods of showers and scattered thunderstorms are expected on \nSun as surface low pressure crosses the region. After an\novernight round of showers, continued clouds could limit sfc \nheating with detrimental impacts to recovery/buoyancy. MLCAPE \ncurrently is progged at 1000 J/kg or less, however, vertical\nwind shear up to 30 kt could help offset meager buoyancy). \n\nIf modest heating between the episodic rain showers on Sun can\noccur, an increase in CAPE within the sheared environment would\nimprove the probability for severe weather to develop. \n\nLocally heavy rain also will be possible as PWATs approach 1.8 \ninches, though the potential dry slot Sunday afternoon ahead of \nthe surface low could limit this potential. The surface cold \nfront is currently expected to cross the area late Sunday/Sunday\nnight.\n\nModel ensembles then indicate a persistent trough across the\nEastern Great Lakes and Ohio Valley region early in the week.\nThis trough should weaken some through the week, though\nsignificant uncertainty exists in exact placement and strength.\nOverall, expect daily chances for diurnally driven convection \nunder this pattern.",
